 \textbf{SQL Dialects.} 
 \textit{Database Management Systems (DBMSs)} underpin modern data-driven applications and expose high-level declarative query languages for data interaction. Although SQL is standardized, real-world DBMSs implement diverse \textit{SQL dialects} with substantial syntactic and semantic variations.
-For example, PostgreSQL parses date strings with \texttt{TO\_DATE} and the format \texttt{\textquotesingle{}YYYY-MM-DD\textquotesingle{}}, whereas DuckDB does not provide \texttt{TO\_DATE} and instead uses \texttt{STRPTIME} with the format \texttt{\textquotesingle{}\%Y-\%m-\%d\textquotesingle{}}. 
+For example, PostgreSQL parses timestamp strings with \texttt{to\_timestamp} and the format \texttt{\textquotesingle{}MMDD\textquotesingle{}}, whereas DuckDB uses \texttt{strptime} with the format \texttt{\textquotesingle{}\%m\%d\textquotesingle{}}. 
 These dialect gaps pose substantial barriers for reusing test assets across different DBMSs.
 
 
