 \textit{Basic Idea of \tool{}.}
 The core idea of \tool{} is to thoroughly decouple logical intent from syntactic representation, shifting from unstable textual translation to more robust structural recursive substitution. 
-As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:motivation}, \tool{} abstracts the dialect-specific \texttt{TO\_DATE} operation into an AQT node labeled $<$\texttt{StrToDate}$>$.
+As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:motivation}, \tool{} abstracts \texttt{to\_timestamp} and its format string into an AQT node labeled $<$\texttt{StrToTimestamp}$>$.
 For multi-statement test cases, it propagates target-side schema and type context in statement order and uses this context together with target-dialect information to instantiate AQT nodes.
 Parser and execution feedback guide the repair of remaining incompatibilities before mutation.
-In this example, \tool{} instantiates $<$\texttt{StrToDate}$>$ with \texttt{STRPTIME} and converts the format string, producing an executable seed that retains the date predicate.
